# Files whose exact bytes are load-bearing must never be translated.
#
# This repository sets core.autocrlf=true, so without an attribute Git rewrites
# LF to CRLF on checkout. That is harmless for source, and fatal for a file that
# something hashes: apps/web/test/real-build-identification-golden.ts reads each
# golden through readBoundedRegularFile with both exactBytes and expectedSha256,
# and those pins are LF byte counts over LF digests.
#
# Demonstrated: `git checkout -- apps/web/test/fixtures/real-build-identification-golden/`
# rewrote every golden to CRLF, took manifest.json from 1028 to 1029 bytes and its
# digest from 23f58599... to c84a4473..., and failed all five golden-reading tests
# with BoundedFileReadError. A fresh clone or a new worktree does the same thing,
# and the failure reads as tampering rather than as a checkout artifact.
#
# One binding is cross-file, which makes the failure harder still to place:
# coverage.json pins pairJudged sha256:f10acf1b..., the digest of its sibling
# pair-judged-truth.json (631 bytes LF, 651 CRLF, digest 6961c65e... translated).
# So the file that fails is not the file Git changed.
#
# The rule is the directory, not the individual file, so a fixture added later
# inherits the protection instead of waiting to be discovered by a red suite.
apps/web/test/fixtures/** -text
scripts/fixtures/** -text

# The first version of this file listed only the digest-pinned goldens, because
# the audit behind it grepped for the two pin helpers. That missed byte
# comparison as a category: scripts/set_6651557_ldraw_source_audit_test.py
# asserts render_source_audit(document) equals the tracked audit's exact bytes,
# so a fresh clone failed `npm run verify` on `...}]}\r\n' != ...}]}\n'` - the
# same hazard, found by cloning rather than by reading.
#
# A generated file is the risky class by construction: something regenerates it
# and something else checks the result matches byte for byte.
packages/catalog/src/quarantine/** -text
*.generated.* -text

# That claim was written as `*.generated.json`, which protected the one file the
# incident named and left the rest of the class translated. A fresh worktree
# therefore still cannot pass `npm run verify`: it fails at the very first stage,
# because packages/protocol/scripts/generate.mjs --check compares bytes against
# types.generated.ts, public-types.generated.ts, validators.generated.js and
# validators.generated.d.ts, and it fails again inside `npm run test`, because
# packages/catalog/src/bundled-geometry-notices.test.ts asserts that
# docs/bundled-geometry-notices.md equals what the catalog renders. Not one of
# those files differs from the index by a character; only the checkout does.
#
# `npm run pin:check` shows what that costs whoever reads the failure. It calls
# the run pin stale and then prints the held and the produced digest side by
# side - and they are the same digest, sha256:366fefbf... twice. The check is
# comparing file bytes while its message talks about hashes, so the error argues
# against its own verdict and sends the reader after a regeneration that will
# change nothing.
#
# So the suffix rule now covers the class rather than one extension of it, and
# the two generated documents that do not carry the suffix are named directly.
THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md -text
docs/bundled-geometry-notices.md -text
